<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhayader</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rhayader is a market town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> in mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in the historic county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radnorshire">Radnorshire</a>, now Powys. It was the centre of the early medieval district of Gwrtheyrnion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd">the Lord Rhys</a> of Deheubarth built a castle there in 1177. In 1843 it saw attacks on tollgates in the Rebecca Riots, and the nearby Elan Valley <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, built from 1893, supply water to Birmingham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhayader.jpg" alt="Rhayader" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velela · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhayader_centre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town lies 20 miles from the source of the Wye on Plynlimon, the highest point of the Cambrian Mountains, at the junction of the A470 and A44 roads, 13 miles north of Builth Wells and 30 miles east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a>. Its Welsh name, Rhaeadr Gwy, means the waterfall on the Wye, but little remains of the fall, which was destroyed in 1780 to make way for the bridge to Cwmdauddwr and the Elan Valley. Cairns and standing stones around the town date from several thousand years BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The route was long a stopping place for travellers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had a camp in the Elan Valley, monks passed between the abbeys of Strata Florida and Abbeycwmhir, and drovers took their livestock to market. Documented history begins with Rhayader Castle in 1177, of which only a dry moat survives. A hoard of gold jewellery of the 1st and 2nd centuries, found nearby in 1899 and known as the Rhayader Hoard, is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. The Old Swan, one of the town's oldest buildings, is recorded in 1676 as one of its two inns.</p><p>During the Rebecca Riots against costly turnpike tolls, local farmers dressed as women demolished six of Rhayader's tollgates, and a Commission of Inquiry followed. In the 1890s Birmingham, 80 miles to the east, chose the Elan Valley as a source of clean water; thousands of workers came to build its dams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, a railway and a village for them were built, and King Edward VII and Queen Alexandra opened the scheme in 1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rhayader is a market town and community within the historic county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radnorshire">Radnorshire</a>, with a population of 2,088 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. By area it is the largest community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and includes the Elan Valley. Its railway station closed on 31 December 1962, and tourism and agriculture are its main industries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhayader” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
